Desired State vs Actual State

✨ Hello DevOps Learners!
Continuing my GitOps learning journey after completing Ansible, today I’m exploring another key GitOps concept: Desired State vs Actual State. 🚀
This concept is what makes GitOps reliable and self-correcting in modern infrastructure management.

🔹 Desired State
 The desired state is the configuration defined in Git.
 It describes how the system should look and behave.
Examples include:
Kubernetes YAML manifests
Deployment configurations
Infrastructure settings
This state is stored in the Git repository and acts as the single source of truth.

🔹 Actual State
 The actual state is the real condition of the system currently running in the cluster or infrastructure environment.
Sometimes the actual state may differ from the desired state due to:
Manual changes
Failed deployments
Configuration errors

🔹 Drift Detection
 GitOps tools constantly compare the desired state (Git) with the actual state (cluster).
If differences are detected, it means configuration drift has occurred.

🔹 Self-Healing Systems
 When drift happens, GitOps tools automatically correct the system state to match what is defined in Git.
 This creates a self-healing infrastructure that maintains consistency.

🔹 Continuous Reconciliation
 GitOps controllers continuously monitor the system and reconcile differences between desired and actual state.
This ensures the infrastructure always stays aligned with Git configurations.

💡 Key Idea:
 GitOps tools continuously ensure that the running system always matches the configuration defined in Git.
